<h1>weak-heap</h1>
<p>
(data structure)
</p>

<p>
<strong>Definition:</strong>
A relaxed <a href="heap.html"><em>heap</em></a> satisfying the following three conditions: (1) every <a href="key.html"><em>key</em></a> in the right subtree of a <a href="node.html"><em>node</em></a> is greater      than the key stored in the node itself,  (2) the <a href="root.html"><em>root</em></a> has no left <a href="child.html"><em>child</em></a>, and (3) <a href="leaf.html"><em>leaves</em></a> are only found on the last two <a href="level.html"><em>levels</em></a> of the tree.
</p>

<p>
<strong>See also</strong>
<a href="weakheapsort.html"><em>weak-heap sort</em></a>.
</p>

<p>
<em>Note:
A weak-heap may be efficiently implemented with an <a href="array.html"><em>array</em></a> a of items and an array r of reverse bits.  The left child of an index i is at index 2i+1-r<sub>i</sub>, and the right child is at index 2i+r<sub>i</sub>. </p> 
<p> This is a minimum weak-heap.  A maximum weak-heap has subtree keys that are less than the node's key.</em>
</p>

<p>Author: <a href="../Other/contrib.html#authorSE">SE</a></p>

